  "answer": "ARP Spoofing works by an adversary intercepting ARP requests on a network. The attacker waits for a device to send an ARP request and then responds with their own MAC address instead of the legitimate device's MAC address. This deceives the victim device into believing it is communicating with the intended device, effectively poisoning its ARP cache. For the attack to be successful, the adversary's reply must arrive faster than the legitimate response from the actual IP address owner. Additionally, adversaries can send gratuitous ARP replies to falsely claim ownership of a specific IP address [Source 2].",
